                <pb facs="0579" n="469" sameAs="frus:frus1928v03/pg469"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/5419: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Eberhardt</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-02-23T14:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">February 23,
                                1928—2 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>43. Your 86 February 21, 11 a.m. Suggestions or movements looking to the
                    selection of a coalition ticket or a single presidential candidate are matters
                    in which the United States is in no way concerned either under the Tipitapa
                    Agreement or otherwise. Any such arrangement would have to be made, if at all,
                    by the political parties involved. The United States has no suggestion to make
                    on that subject. You may deny categorically that the individuals mentioned <pb
                        facs="0580" n="470" xml:id="pg470"/> in the first paragraph of your message
                    have at any time expressed the views attributed to them. Please make our
                    position clear to all concerned.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
